data.content:
Here are the TUnit equivalents:

```csharp
Assert.That(actual).IsEqualTo(expected);
Assert.That(order.IsValid).IsTrue();
Assert.That(result.Error).IsNull();
Assert.That(handler).IsTypeOf<IOrderHandler>();
Assert.That(processedIds).Contains(42);
var ex = Assert.Throws<InvalidOperationException>(() => processor.Run());
Assert.That(ex.Message).IsEqualTo("Order is empty");
```

Key differences from MSTest:
- TUnit uses a fluent `Assert.That(value).IsX()` pattern instead of `Assert.IsX(value)`
- `AreEqual(expected, actual)` becomes `Assert.That(actual).IsEqualTo(expected)` (note the argument order flip)
- `CollectionAssert.Contains` becomes `Assert.That(collection).Contains(item)`
- `Assert.ThrowsException<T>` becomes `Assert.Throws<T>`
